{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>In that day YHWH cut a covenant with Abram, saying: To your seed I have given.</b> After He mentioned above the gift of the land twice — לזרעך אתן, לזרעך אתננה — in the future tense, He now says נתתי in the past tense, for He called to mind the gift that He had already given him. And the word נתתי is like נשבעתי ('I have sworn'), for the gift of HaShem by speech alone is accounted like an oath; hence it says everywhere, אשר נשבע ה׳ לאבותיכם. I have already explained this in connection with the verse (Genesis 9): את קשתי. And after this gift, which is the oath, came the covenant of בין הבתרים ('between the pieces'), for such is the way of those who cut a covenant: they swear first and afterward cut the covenant. The covenant is that they pass between the pieces of the animal, in the manner of what is written (Jeremiah 34): העגל אשר כרתו לשנים ויעברו בין בתריו. This covenant was like a חרם (a ban/devotion), and it is the ratification of the oath already sworn, as if to say: may he who transgresses the oath be cut up like this animal. Therefore Onkelos translates it ברית קיימא — the ratification of the oath sworn beforehand. This is what Avimelekh said to Isaac: תהי נא אלה בינותינו בינינו ובינך ונכרתה ברית עמך — he mentioned the oath first and afterward the covenant. A covenant of this kind does not befall the Shekhinah, perish the thought, but the intention in this covenant when it is with the Holy One, blessed be He, is: just as it is impossible for the pieces to return to what they were, so it is impossible for His gift, blessed be He, to be annulled." "<b>From the river of Egypt to the great river.</b> Scripture mentions in this passage the gift of the land three times. <b>First:</b> when Abraham began to enter the land, He said to him: לזרעך אתן את הארץ הזאת — yet He did not explain the gift, for what reason would He say לזרעך אתן את הארץ הזאת when he had gone only as far as the place of Shechem, as far as אלון מורה, alone? <b>A second time:</b> He said to him: שא נא עיניך וראה מן המקום אשר אתה שם צפונה ונגבה — and it is known that a person's sight does not reach far, but the intention was to give him the directions as seen by his eyes, and He said: כי את כל הארץ אשר אתה רואה לך אתננה, and added: ולזרעך עד עולם, and also promised him that He would multiply his seed like the dust of the earth. <b>The third time:</b> He explained to him the boundaries of the land — this is מנהר מצרים עד הנהר הגדול נהר פרת. And He said לזרעך נתתי in the past tense, for until now He had said לזרעך אתננה in the future tense, and the intention was to say that He would cut a covenant with him concerning the gift He had already given him. Thus explained the Ramban, of blessed memory." "<b>And the reason</b> He called the Euphrates הנהר הגדול ('the great river'), though Scripture counts it fourth among the rivers of Eden, is because it is attached to the land of Israel — and so the proverb says: a servant who attaches himself to the king is called 'black,' and they bow to you. And since the Holy Land is the choicest of the inhabited world, and the Temple that is there is called הבית הגדול ('the great house'), and the one who serves there is called הכהן הגדול ('the great priest'), and the Name that dwells within it is called האל הגדול ('the great El') — and so King Solomon, peace be upon him, said in Chronicles (2 Chronicles 2): והבית אשר אני בונה גדול כי גדול אלהינו מכל האלהים — therefore Scripture also called the river that surrounds it הנהר הגדול. And as for His having said <b>לזרעך נתתי</b> regarding those ten nations, when neither Ishmael nor Isaac had yet been born — this is not to be understood as a promise to Ishmael the son of the maidservant, for he is not the root of his seed; rather, it is a promise to Isaac the son of the mistress, who is the root of his seed, in the manner of what the Holy One, blessed be He, promised him: <b>כי ביצחק יקרא לך זרע</b> (Genesis 21)."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:12:53.891721829Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}